{"name":"Aztec Warrior","hex":"#bb0066","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"warrior","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who is actively engaged in battle, conflict or warfare; a soldier or combatant.","A person who is aggressively, courageously, or energetically involved in an activity, such as athletics."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warrior"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eagle warrior","blocks":[{"text":"Eagle warriors or eagle knights were a special class of infantry soldier in the Aztec army, one of the two leading military special forces orders in Aztec society, the other being the Jaguar warriors. They were a type of Aztec warrior called a cuāuhocēlōtl. The word cuāuhocēlōtl derives from the eagle warrior cuāuhtli and the jaguar warrior ocēlōtl. These military orders were made up of the bravest soldiers of noble birth and those who had taken the greatest number of prisoners in battle. Of all of the Aztec warriors, they were the most feared. Eagle warriors, along with the jaguar warriors, were the only such classes that did not restrict access solely to the nobility, as commoners or, in Nahuatl, \"mācēhualli\" were occasionally admitted for special merit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Aztec_Eagle_Warrior_%289781975233%29.jpg/330px-Aztec_Eagle_Warrior_%289781975233%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_warrior","wikidata":"Q250895"},{"title":"Jaguar warrior","blocks":[{"text":"Jaguar warriors or jaguar knights were members of the Aztec military elite, similar to the eagle warriors. They were a type of Aztec warrior called a cuāuhocēlōtl"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Jaguar_warrior.jpg/330px-Jaguar_warrior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaguar_warrior","wikidata":"Q636453"},{"title":"Aztecs","blocks":[{"text":"The Aztecs were a Mesoamerican civilization that flourished in central Mexico from about 1300 to 1521. The Aztec people included different ethnic groups of central Mexico, particularly those who spoke Nahuatl. Aztec culture was organized into city-states (altepetl), some of which joined to form alliances, political confederations, or empires. The Aztec Empire was a confederation of three city-states established in 1427: Tenochtitlan, Tetzcoco, and Tlacopan. Although the term Aztecs is often narrowly restricted to the Mexica of Tenochtitlan, it is also broadly used to refer to Nahua polities or peoples of central Mexico in the pre-Hispanic era, as well as the Spanish colonial era (1521–1821)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Aztec_Empire.png/330px-Aztec_Empire.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aztecs","wikidata":"Q12542"},{"title":"Warrior","blocks":[{"text":"A warrior is a guardian specializing in combat or warfare, especially within the context of a tribal or clan-based warrior culture society that recognizes a separate warrior aristocracy, class, or caste."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warrior","wikidata":"Q1250916"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg/500px-Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg/1280px-Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg/500px-Florentine_Codex_IX_Aztec_Warriors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Eagle warrior (left) d